\'93Found him!\'94 Sman smiled with the satisfaction of the thrill of a chase nearing its fruition. He trained his rifle down and steadied his aim.\
Alexander kicked the rifle. The particle beam wen wide and blew out an ambulance tyre. Alexander kicked again. The rifle clattered on the cruiser\'92s landing skids and fell to the street. In a second, the lead cop was standing chest-to-chest with Alexander. \'93What in the Alfred Deakin was that?\'94\
\'93Sorry.\'94 Alexander fumbled. \'93Sciatic tic. Old publications relations injury.\'94\
\'93What the AF, Lieutenant?\'94 the cop blustered, and handed Sman his pistol. \'93Take the shot, corporal.\'94\
\'93Yes. sir.\'94\
\'93You can\'92t shoot him.\'94 Alexander was suddenly assertive.\
\'93And just why the Edmund Barton not, Lieutenant?\'94\
Alexander worked his jaw and compressed his lips. He couldn\'92t admit to knowing Cracker, certainly not in this situation. \'93Take them to a hospital,\'94 Alexander said.\
The cop\'92s eyes bulged. \'93I knew bringing you on this mission as a figurehead was a bad idea. Take the shot, Corporal.\'94\
The corporal aimed the pistol. Alexander surprised the cop, and shoved him off his feet. Before Sman could steady his aim, Alexander planted his foot between Sman\'92s shoulder blade and assisted him out of the cruisers. Sman died with an arrested wail in his throat, his brains dashed out on smouldering asphalt.\
The lead cop waved the others back from reacting, wiped his mouth and considered how he\'92d end this showtime impersonator.\
\'93You know,\'94 Alexander said, \'93for the sake of the demographics, given the way the corporal mopped up after you grunts, we could classify him as a cleaner, couldn\'92t we?\'94\
A smile cracked the cop\'92s face. \'93Son of a sow,\'94 he said, seeing Alexander as if for the first time.
